f the mouse telencephalon. Dev Biol. 2005;279(1):155&#x2013;68.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15708565</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Arai Y, Cwetsch AW, Coppola E, Cipriani S, Nishihara H, Kanki H, et al. Evolutionary Gain of Dbx1 Expression Drives Subplate Identity in the Cerebral Cortex. Cell reports. 2019;29(3):645&#x2013;58 e5.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">31618633</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Thomsen MS, Routhe LJ, Moos T. The vascular basement membrane in the healthy and pathological brain. J Cereb Blood Flow Metab. 2017;37(10):3300&#x2013;17.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC5624399</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28753105</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hartwig C, Veske A, Krejcova S, Rosenberger G, Finckh U. Plexin B3 promotes neurite outgrowth, interacts homophilically, and interacts with Rin. BMC Neurosci. 2005;6:53.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1215486</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16122393</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhu B, Chen C, Xue G, Moyzis RK, Dong Q, Chen C, et al. The SEMA5A gene is associated with hippocampal volume, and their interaction is associated with performance on Raven&#x2019;s Progressive Matrices. NeuroImage. 2014;88:181&#x2013;7.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24291503</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bloch MH, Leckman JF. Clinical course of Tourette syndrome. Journal of psychosomatic research. 2009;67(6):497&#x2013;501.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3974606</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19913654</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Leckman JF, Peterson B. The pathogenesis of Tourette&#x2019;s syndrome: role od epigenetic factors active in early CNS development. Biol Psychiatry. 1993;34:425&#x2013;7.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8268326</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Peterson B, Riddle MA, Cohen DJ, Katz LD, Smith JC, Hardin MT, et al. Reduced basal ganglia volumes in Tourette&#x2019;s syndrome using three-dimensional reconstruction techniques from magnetic resonance images. Neurology. 1993;43(5):941&#x2013;9.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8492950</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Heinz A, Knable MB, Wolf SS, Jones DW, Gorey JG, Hyde TM, et al. Tourette&#x2019;s syndrome: [I-123]beta-CIT SPECT correlates of vocal tic severity. Neurology. 1998;51(4):1069&#x2013;74.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9781531</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Muller-Vahl KR, Meyer GJ, Knapp WH, Emrich HM, Gielow P, Brucke T, et al. Serotonin transporter binding in Tourette Syndrome. Neurosci Lett. 2005;385(2):120&#x2013;5.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15936877</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wong DF, Brasic JR, Singer HS, Schretlen DJ, Kuwabara H, Zhou Y, et al. Mechanisms of dopaminergic and serotonergic neurotransmission in Tourette syndrome: clues from an in vivo neurochemistry study with PET. Neuropsychopharmacology. 2008;33(6):1239&#x2013;51.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3696501</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">17987065</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Muller-Vahl KR, Szejko N, Wilke F, Jakubovski E, Geworski L, Bengel F, et al. Serotonin transporter binding is increased in Tourette syndrome with Obsessive Compulsive Disorder. Scientific reports. 2019;9(1):972.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6353942</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30700759</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rallu M, Corbin JG, Fishell G. Parsing the prosencephalon. Nat Rev Neurosci. 2002;3(12):943&#x2013;51.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12461551</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Storm EE, Garel S, Borello U, Hebert JM, Martinez S, McConnell SK, et al. Dose-dependent functions of Fgf8 in regulating telencephalic patterning centers. Development. 2006;133(9):1831&#x2013;44.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16613831</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wen X, Lai CK, Evangelista M, Hongo JA, de Sauvage FJ, Scales SJ. Kinetics of hedgehog-dependent full-length Gli3 accumulation in primary cilia and subsequent degradation. Mol Cell Biol. 2010;30(8):1910&#x2013;22.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2849461</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20154143</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Haycraft CJ, Banizs B, Aydin-Son Y, Zhang Q, Michaud EJ, Yoder BK. Gli2 and Gli3 localize to cilia and require the intraflagellar transport protein polaris for processing and function. PLoS Genet. 2005;1(4):e53.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1270009</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16254602</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kim J, Kato M, Beachy PA. Gli2 trafficking links Hedgehog-dependent activation of Smoothened in the primary cilium to transcriptional activation in the nucleus. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A. 2009;106(51):21666&#x2013;71.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2790365</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19996169</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Li J, Wang C, Wu C, Cao T, Xu G, Meng Q, et al. PKA-mediated Gli2 and Gli3 phosphorylation is inhibited by Hedgehog signaling in cilia and reduced in Talpid3 mutant. Dev Biol. 2017;429(1):147&#x2013;57.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC5831674</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28673820</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee B, Panda S, Lee HY. Primary Ciliary Deficits in the Dentate Gyrus of Fragile X Syndrome. Stem cell reports. 2020;15(2):454&#x2013;66.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7419715</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">32735823</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">40702288</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>27</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>30</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1559-1182</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>62</Volume><Issue>12</Issue><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Dec</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Molecular neurobiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Mol Neurobiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Defining the Cis-Regulatory Elements of SCN1A in GABAergic Interneurons.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>15730</StartPage><EndPage>15740</EndPage><MedlinePgn>15730-15740</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12035-025-05209-5</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Dravet syndrome (DS) is a severe neurodevelopmental disorder associated with physical and cognitive impairments, often attributed to mutations in the SCN1A gene. However, gene regulatory features that modulate SCN1A expression found in vivo and in vitro models remain elusive. This study elucidates cell type-specific cis-regulatory elements (CRE) regulating SCN1A expression in cortical fast-spiking GABAergic inhibitory interneurons, the predominantly affected cell type in DS. We establish a differentiation protocol from human induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) for both healthy control and DS patient-derived lines, followed by single-cell 5' RNA-seq at different stages of interneuron maturation. Through this analysis, we identify interneuron-enriched transcriptional start sites (TSSs) in cell type-restricted promoters regulated by a distinct set of transcription factors. Furthermore, we investigate SCN1A anti-sense (AS) transcripts and emphasize the importance of accurate 5' gene annotation in the context of developing targeted gene therapeutics. This work highlights the complexities and dynamics of studying DS at the molecular level and the need for comprehensive gene regulation analysis in vitro and ex vivo to enable precision medicine therapeutic approaches for DS.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2025. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Luginb&#xfc;hl</LastName><ForeName>Joachim</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Prabhu</LastName><ForeName>Anika V</ForeName><Initials>AV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yip</LastName><ForeName>Chi Wai</ForeName><Initials>CW</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ando</LastName><ForeName>Yoshinari</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Leon</LastName><ForeName>Julio</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yasuzawa</LastName><ForeName>Kayoko</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hon</LastName><ForeName>Chung Chau</ForeName><Initials>CC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Genome Information Analysis, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Moody</LastName><ForeName>Jonathan</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Genome Information Analysis, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Roudnicky</LastName><ForeName>Filip</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pharma Research and Early Development, Therapeutic Modalities, F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd, 4070, Basel, Switzerland.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kremer</LastName><ForeName>Thomas</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Pharma Research and Early Development, Neuroscience and Rare Diseases, F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd, 4070, Basel, Switzerland.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Shin</LastName><ForeName>Jay W</ForeName><Initials>JW</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Advanced Genomics Circuit, RIKEN Center for Integrative Medical Sciences (IMS), 230-0045, Yokohama, Japan. jay_shin@gis.a-star.edu.sg.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Genome Institute of Singapore (GIS), Agency for Science, Technology and Research (A&#x2009;&#x2217;&#x2009;STAR), 60 Biopolis Street, 138672, Sing